{align:justify|Introduction}

{align:justify|Many people know that whoever sincerely makes a Buddha statue gains great merit, yet few know how vast that merit is. It is in this very SŪTRA ON MAKING BUDDHA IMAGES that the Buddha teaches the matter clearly.}

{align:justify|However, one must make the Buddha statue with a sincere heart, endowing it with fine and dignified auspicious marks. If complete perfection cannot be achieved, then at the very least it must display features expressing Great Wisdom, Great Compassion, and Great Liberation — or, as this sūtra teaches: it must possess the two marks, the white tuft between the eyebrows and the fleshy crown on the head, so that anyone who looks upon it knows it is a Buddha statue and readily gives rise to reverent faith; only then is there merit. Conversely, if a Buddha statue displays none of the Buddha's marks, so that onlookers cannot even tell it is a Buddha statue, no faithful confidence arises and it is even regarded with contempt, then such statue-making naturally brings no merit, and at times even incurs offense.}

{align:justify|Therefore, to help foster a correct and profound understanding of the merit of making Buddha statues, I praise and joyfully introduce to you, fellow Buddhists, this translation of the “SŪTRA ON THE MERIT OF MAKING BUDDHA IMAGES” by Bhikkhu Thích Thiện Thông.}

{align:right|Từ Đàm Pagoda, May 5, 1993}

{align:right|The Most Venerable Thích Thiện Siêu}

{align:right|FOREWORD}

{align:justify|Fashioning images of Buddhas or images of Bodhisattvas is a deed of noble meaning that creates a cause of immense merit and blessing! Thus for more than 2,500 years, since the Buddha entered nirvāṇa, his monastic and lay disciples — from India, China, and Japan to Buddhist lands the world over, including Vietnam — countless people have created images of the Buddhas and Bodhisattvas to behold, venerate, and make offerings to.}

{align:justify|Before briefly outlining the merit and blessings of making Buddha statues, let us travel back in time to learn where the making of Buddha images originated and who first resolved to make a Buddha image.}

{align:justify|By a fortunate chance granted to us: in the rains retreat of 1992, after translating the SŪTRA ON THE FIELD OF BLESSINGS OF ALL VIRTUES and the SŪTRA ON INCOMPARABILITY (sūtras on the merit of taking refuge in the Three Jewels), we came upon the MAHĀYĀNA SŪTRA ON THE MERIT OF MAKING BUDDHA IMAGES, a sūtra in two fascicles catalogued as number 694 in the Chinese Taishō Tripiṭaka. Having read it through, we were overjoyed, for we recognized novelties never before encountered in any Vietnamese translation of a Buddhist sūtra. Yet it may be said that in the book “Miraculous Responses of the Three Jewels,” Venerable Thích Thiền Tâm did translate a few brief passages touching on King Udayana's making of a Buddha statue.}

{align:justify|According to this MAHĀYĀNA SŪTRA ON THE MERIT OF MAKING BUDDHA IMAGES, when the Buddha was still in the world, King Udayana, ruler of the land of Kauśāmbī, was the first to use fragrant red sandalwood to fashion an image of Buddha Śākyamuni.}

{align:justify|What then prompted King Udayana to make a Buddha image? The motive that moved him was as follows:}

{align:justify|After nearly forty-nine years of teaching the Dharma to liberate beings, during one ninety-day rains retreat (likely his last), the Buddha was absent from the human world: he had ascended to the Trāyastriṃśa Heaven to preach the sūtras, convert the gods, and bring his mother, Lady Māyā, to attain sainthood. King Udayana could find the Buddha nowhere, and his longing heart ached with extreme yearning, until in grief and sorrow he neglected affairs of state for many days and cared nothing for royal pleasures. Not knowing how else to see the Buddha and ease his longing, he thought of making a Buddha statue.}

{align:justify|The king summoned the country's renowned sculptors to make a Buddha statue, but each declined, daring not to accept, for they thought: the Buddha's physical form is wondrously adorned with fine marks, and should the statue turn out flawed, they would incur offense and lose their good name. At that time the god Viśvakarman, traditionally said to be the god of craftsmen, transformed himself into a carpenter; bringing his tools, he undertook to fashion the Buddha's image to aid the king. Displaying his utmost skill, Viśvakarman completed the Buddha statue in a single day: seven cubits tall, purple-gold in color, complete with fine marks, so that whoever beheld it knew it was a Buddha statue.}

{align:justify|When King Udayana finished making the Buddha statue, its fame spread to neighboring lands, and the kings of great nations — King Prasenajit, King Ajātaśatru, and King Nghiêm Xí — all brought offerings to behold and venerate it, and all praised King Udayana's deed.}

{align:justify|When the three months ended and the Buddha descended again to Jambudvīpa, countless gods of the six heavens of the desire realm and the eighteen heavens of the form realm all presented offerings to the Buddha and escorted him back to the human world. Disciples monastic and lay, from at least four lands and every walk of life, gathered at the city of Sāṃkāśya to welcome the Buddha. A supremely magnificent scene, unprecedented in history, unfolded then — a divine meeting of Heaven and humankind, never before seen among mortals. All who sincerely brought offerings to the Buddha accomplished blessed merit and planted causes for liberation in this life and lives to come; many followers of heterodox sects thereby abandoned wrong paths for right ones and took refuge in the Three Jewels. Thus the Buddha fulfilled his original vow to liberate beings by appearing in this world. Hence the SŪTRA OF BEQUEATHED TEACHINGS records: “Those fit to be liberated I have liberated; those not yet liberated, I have already planted causes for their future liberation...” This clearly reveals the boundless compassion with which Buddha Śākyamuni, our World-Honored One, rescues sentient beings.}

{align:justify|So that beings of later ages may clearly understand the blessings and merit of making Buddha statues, the Bodhisattva Maitreya, on behalf of the whole assembly of the holy and the ordinary, asked the Buddha about the merit of King Udayana's statue-making, and the Buddha in turn spoke of the supreme rewards and merit of those who with sincere, pure faith fashion Buddha statues. This sūtra records those inconceivable rewards of merit in full. Below we quote a few passages to give you a general idea:}

{align:justify|“Maitreya! If someone embroiders a Buddha image with silk threads, or casts one in gold, silver, copper, iron, lead, or zinc, or carves one from fragrant sandalwood, or fashions one from pearls, shells, brocade and woven silks, red earth, cement, plaster, clay, or various woods... using such materials according to their means to make a Buddha statue, even one as small as a thumb, so that others who see it know it is a Buddha statue — that person's blessed reward I shall now describe:}

{align:justify|“Maitreya! Such persons, though still drifting through birth and death, will never be born into desperately poor families, nor into lowly or ignoble lineages, nor lonely and remote on a country's fringes. Nor will they be born into wrong-view families, traders' or merchants' homes, or butchers' households, down even to lowly lines, impure brothels, or the households of heterodox ascetics holding wrong views — unless through the power of compassionate vows; apart from that, they will never be born into such homes.}

{align:justify|“Such a person is often born into the clan of a mighty wheel-turning sage-king, or into a Brahmin line of pure conduct, or into a wealthy house free of grave offenses. Wherever born, they always meet Buddhas and reverently serve and make offerings to them; or they become kings who uphold the true Dharma, teaching it to others without going against the path, or mighty wheel-turning sage-kings. Wherever born, they are always men, never taking a woman's body. Nor do they take the base body of a hermaphrodite or one of dual sex; the body they receive at birth is never ugly. Their eyes are never blind or defective, their ears never deaf or misshapen, their noses never crooked or broken, their mouths never twisted, their lips never drooping nor pursed, their teeth never gapped, missing, black, or yellow, their tongues never short or drawn back, their necks never goitered, their backs never hunched, their skin never blotchy, their hands never stubby, their feet never lame — never too gaunt, never too stout, neither too tall nor too short — none of the unlovely features at all...”}

{align:justify|The above is but a small excerpt on the rewards of lineage and bodily form for makers of Buddha statues. Beyond these blessings there are further fruits: the eradication of all manner of karmic hindrances, such as the five heinous transgressions, the ten unwholesome deeds, the four grave violations, and so forth — in short, the heaviest evil karma a human being can incur. Yet if one sincerely resolves to make a statue, repents, and vows never to repeat the offense, those evil deeds and their fruits are all destroyed.}

{align:justify|Why is the merit of making statues so great?}

{align:justify|Because all Tathāgatas, from the time they first aroused the aspiration to practice until attaining Buddhahood, passed through countless asaṃkhyeya kalpas, accumulating measureless blessings and accomplishing boundless merit and wisdom, and with that merit they ever protect sentient beings of pure faith. That is why making Buddha statues can wipe away the gravest evil karma.}

{align:justify|Moreover, the power of mind in each sentient being — in each of us — is itself inconceivable, inherently replete with Ganges'-sands of merit and wondrous functions. If we constantly contemplate the Buddhas' meritorious virtues, constantly regard the Tathāgatas as endowed with the thirty-two fine marks and eighty beauties, the ten powers, the four fearlessnesses, the six perfections, and great kindness, great compassion, and great wisdom, then in the very act of contemplating the Buddhas' virtues we activate the seeds of our own inconceivable meritorious cause. Through the power of this perfuming, even the heaviest evil karma and adverse fruits can be transformed. We contemplate the fine marks upon our own physical bodies; once the seed exists, the fruit will arise, and when the fruit arises we shall possess those fine marks. This principle is utterly true and never fails.}

{align:justify|In short, by contemplating the Buddhas' virtues externally, we accomplish the merit of the Buddha-mind within our own minds; and by contemplating the Buddha's virtues within our minds, we accomplish blessed rewards of body and circumstance externally. This is a wondrous principle called the Great Vehicle, and the making of Buddha statues belongs to that wondrous principle — so much so that the sūtra on statue-making is itself called the MAHĀYĀNA SŪTRA ON THE MERIT OF MAKING BUDDHA IMAGES.}

{align:justify|This sūtra comprises two fascicles, composed in a sharp, outstanding literary style. Beyond teaching the merit and blessings accomplished through statue-making, it also clearly reveals the Buddha's supremely skillful means: how, to teach beings, he manifests undergoing hindrances and hardships. Apart from the most learned, few among us Buddhists know of this.}

{align:justify|The sūtra also clearly teaches the causes by which beings must take female bodies, hermaphrodite or dual-sex bodies, be born in times or places without the Buddhadharma, or be born in wild borderlands — truly matters hard to encounter and hard to understand.}

{align:justify|Seeing that this sūtra holds such great value, we have endeavored to translate it into Vietnamese to offer to our present age and to contribute our small share to the Vietnamese translation of the Tripiṭaka undertaken by Vietnamese Buddhism.}

{align:justify|Moreover, since a sūtra of such literary and doctrinal worth also contains a rich treasury of Sino-Vietnamese terms and expressions that may aid those studying literary Chinese today, we present both the Chinese-character text and its Vietnamese reading, for the convenience of those who delight in chanting it.}

{align:justify|Though we have tried our best, our work and understanding remain shallow and flawed in many places; yet as we have understood, so have we recorded it, and we sincerely hope the learned will instruct us.}

{align:justify|Respectfully recorded by the translator,}

{align:justify|On the Ullambana season of the Quý Dậu year, 1993}

{align:justify|Bhikkhu Thích Thiện Thông.}

{align:center|**THE MAHĀYĀNA SŪTRA ON THE MERIT OF MAKING BUDDHA IMAGES**}



{align:center|**Under the Great Tang Dynasty: The Land of Khotan**}



{align:center|**Rendered into Vietnamese by Bhikkhu Thích Thiện Thông of Vietnam.**}

{align:justify|Thus have I heard: At one time the Buddha was dwelling in the Trāyastriṃśa Heaven, beneath the Pāriyātraka tree[1], together with an infinite assembly of great bhikkhus and infinite great Bodhisattvas, led by the Great Bodhisattva Maitreya.}

{align:justify|At that time, the World-Honored One kept the rains retreat of three months in that heaven, teaching the Dharma to his mother, bringing much benefit to the gods; countless gods left suffering behind and gained liberation. Countless gods were steeped in the teaching and obtained great blessed fruit. Among those gods was a young god whose lifespan was nearly exhausted, and the five signs of decline appeared[2]. Yet through the power of hearing the Dharma, after his death he was reborn again in this very heaven, leaving the evil paths behind for good.}

{align:justify|At that time, in Jambudvīpa there was no Buddha. It was as on a dark night full of stars but with no moon, as a land with no king, as a house with no master — all amusements and entertainments ceased entirely. Living beings then seemed orphaned, with no refuge to turn to. Toward the Buddha, everyone's heart bore a yearning love and deep sorrow, as at losing father and mother, as an arrow shot into the heart; together they went to where the Buddha had stayed, only to find groves, courtyards, and terraces silent and empty — everywhere vacant, with no Buddha — and their grief and longing only grew, unassuaged.}

{align:justify|At that time, King Udayana, dwelling in his palace, bore a constant sorrow, ever moved and yearning for the Buddha; the king cared nothing for his queens, his maidens, or his pleasures, and thought to himself: “In my present grief I shall not live long! How can I see the Buddha before I cast off this life?” Then he thought again: “When one loves someone dearly but cannot meet them, seeing the place where that person once stayed, or seeing someone exactly resembling them, may lessen the sorrow.” Pondering further, he reflected: “If I now go to the place where the Buddha once stayed[3] and do not see him, I shall surely weep in grief, moved to the point of death! I survey the world and find no one whose bodily marks, blessings, wisdom could equal the Tathāgata — how could I meet such a one to ease my sorrow?”}

{align:justify|Having aroused this thought, the king reflected further: “Now I shall commission the making of a Buddha image, to venerate and make offerings to.” But the king considered: “If the statue I make does not resemble the Buddha, I fear it will bring me measureless offense.” Then he thought again: “Suppose all the wise of the world[4] joined together to extol the Buddha's virtues, they still could not exhaust them. If any person praises him according to their share, that person still gains measureless blessing. So it is with me now: I should make a statue according to my own share.”}

{align:justify|The king at once commanded that all the skilled craftsmen of the land assemble. When all had gathered, the king told them:}

{align:justify|- Whoever can make an image of the Buddha for me, I shall repay and reward with jewels and treasures.}

{align:justify|The master craftsmen all memorialized the king:}

{align:justify|- What the king commands is truly a difficult task. The wondrous fine marks of the Tathāgata have no equal in this world. How could we fashion a Buddha image? Even if the god Viśvakarman[5] himself were to make it, he could never perfectly resemble the Tathāgata. If we accept the king's command to make a Buddha image, we might at best imitate in small part the curled hair and the white tuft; as for the other radiant, beautiful marks, full of august dignity — who could fashion them? The Buddha will soon return from heaven to the human world; should the statue turn out flawed, our good names would be ruined. We venture to say we dare not do it.}

{align:justify|King Udayana then replied:}

{align:justify|- My mind is resolved; do not refuse! As a man parched with thirst seeks living water to drink — would he refuse to drink only because he cannot drink it all?}

{align:justify|Hearing the king's words, the skilled craftsmen all knelt before him and memorialized:}

{align:justify|- We shall obey the command, but we beg the king to grant us this night to reflect; tomorrow morning we shall come to work.}

{align:justify|They further memorialized:}

{align:justify|- As the king makes this statue, it should be of pure sandalwood[6], whose grain is fine and whose substance is most durable. And should the Buddha's image be seated or standing, and of what height?}

{align:justify|The king put the question to his ministers. One well-informed minister came forth and memorialized:}

{align:justify|- The king should make the Tathāgata seated. Why? Because all Buddhas attain great awakening seated, turn the wheel of Dharma seated, display great miracles seated, subdue heterodox teachers seated, and perform great Buddha-deeds seated... all seated. Therefore the Buddha's image should be made seated in the cross-legged posture[7] upon a lion throne.}

{align:justify|At that very moment, the god Viśvakarman, seeing this affair from afar and discerning the king's wish to make a Buddha statue, pondered through the night: “My skill and understanding are most marvelous and rare; in all the world none equals me. If I make a statue for the king, it may somewhat resemble the Buddha[8].” He then transformed himself into a carpenter carrying sharp tools. Just as dawn broke, the man arrived to stand beside the palace gate, telling the gatekeeper to report clearly to the king: “I now wish to make a statue for the king; in craftsmanship none equals me. I humbly beg the king to appoint no other.”}

{align:justify|Hearing these words, the king was overjoyed and ordered the man invited in. Observing his bearing and conduct, the king knew him a master craftsman, and thought to himself: “How could such a man exist in the world? Could this be the god Viśvakarman or his disciple come hither?” Thereupon the king removed the jewel necklace he wore, and with his own hands draped it around Viśvakarman's neck, promising besides to reward his labor with all manner of jewels.}

{align:justify|The king at once went with the keeper of the storehouse into the treasury to select fragrant woods, and with his own shoulders bore them[9] forth to hand to the divine craftsman, telling him:}

{align:justify|- Excellent, Venerable Sir! Use this wood to make a statue for me, so that it resembles the Buddha's form.}

{align:justify|Then the divine craftsman addressed the king:}

{align:justify|- Though my craft is indeed foremost, when it comes to fashioning the Buddha's image, I can never exhaust its perfection.}

{align:justify|It is as if a man took charcoal to sketch the sun and claimed the likeness true — that would be absurd. So it is even with fashioning a Buddha statue from pure gold.}

{align:justify|There are heterodox teachers who commonly proclaim: “Brahmā can create the world[10],” yet even they could not fully fashion the fine marks of the Buddha's form. Only my craft is supreme in the world, and therefore I now fashion it for the king. This morning is the eighth day of the month, just when the Puṣya and Punarvasu stars appear. When the Buddha was newly born, these same signs of response appeared. Today is auspicious; let the work begin.”}

{align:justify|Having spoken, Viśvakarman took up his adze to hew the wood, and the sound of his hewing rose all the way to the Trāyastriṃśa Heaven, reaching the Buddha. Through the Buddha's spiritual power, wherever the sound echoed, whatever beings heard the chisel-stroke had their defilements and afflictions wiped away.}

{align:justify|Then the Tathāgata smiled, in many ways praising the king's merit, and even from afar bestowed upon the king the prediction of supreme, right, and perfect awakening.}

{align:justify|At that time, the Lord of the Trāyastriṃśa Heaven addressed the Buddha:}

{align:justify|- World-Honored One! In the present world, has anyone in past kalpas ever made a Buddha statue?}

{align:justify|The Buddha told the heavenly lord Śakra:}

{align:justify|- All who ever made statues of Buddhas belong to the past, and have long since been liberated. Among this heavenly host here not one remains behind (stranded), let alone elsewhere. Only the son of Vaiśravaṇa of the North, named Na Lý Sa Bà, once long ago made a Bodhisattva statue; through that blessing he later became a king named Bimbisāra. And through meeting me, he has now been born in heaven with great power, departed from the evil paths for good.}

{align:justify|The bhikkhus Uruvilvā Kāśyapa, Gayā Kāśyapa, and Nadī Kāśyapa[11] — all three in past kalpas once repaired an old Buddha shrine, and through those causes have gained liberation forever. Gavāṃpati was once an ox; seeking grass and water, he wandered around a monastery, grazing among the bamboo groves, and upon seeing the Buddha's venerable countenance gave rise to a joyful heart — through that blessing he is now liberated. Śibi once held a jeweled parasol as an offering to a Buddha statue. Anuruddha lit a single lamp, also as an offering. Du Bệ Ma once swept a Buddha hall. A Bà Ma Na lit lamps before a Buddha statue to add brightness, and the bhikkhu Nanda, cherishing the venerable bearing, bathed it with perfumed water, and so on... Countless arhats such as these all once presented trifling gifts before Buddha statues. Down to the least, like Na Già Ba La, who before a statue's throne used a speck of red and gold pigment to paint a statue as an offering... Through such blessings all have left suffering behind and gained liberation.}

{align:justify|The Buddha continued:}

{align:justify|- Śakra, Lord of Gods! If anyone, while my teaching has not yet vanished[12], makes a Buddha statue, all shall gain liberation in Maitreya Buddha's first assembly.}

{align:justify|If any being, not for their own sake seeking escape, but wishing to realize supreme awakening, makes a Buddha statue, know that this is the true cause of the thirty-two marks, enabling them swiftly to become a Buddha. At that time, King Udayana pondered in his heart: “How can the statue I commission be swiftly completed?” Having thought so, he said to the divine craftsman:}

{align:justify|- Venerable Sir, strive with all your heart to bring the work swiftly to completion, that I may soon behold and venerate it.}

{align:justify|Then the god Viśvakarman displayed his utmost skill, wholly devoted and unflagging, and in less than a day the statue was complete! Seated cross-legged, seven cubits tall, its face, hands, and feet all purple-gold in color.}

{align:justify|Seeing the statue complete, its fine marks dignified and adorned, King Udayana's heart gave rise to pure, clear faith and he realized the acquiescence of conformity[13]. Having realized acquiescence, the king rejoiced all the more; all his karmic hindrances and sorrows melted away like mist before the risen sun — save for one karma still undergone in his present body[14], for having once spoken evil words against a holy person. The king then bestowed all manner of precious jewels and rarities upon the divine craftsman, but the craftsman addressed the king:}

{align:justify|- As the king makes this statue, my heart rejoices in sympathy; I vow to cultivate this blessing together with the king. What the king now grants, I dare not accept. If we would exchange gifts, pray wait for another auspicious day. Having spoken, that very night the god returned to heaven.}

{align:justify|Soon after, the kings of great lands, such as King Ajātaśatru[15]... Before this, all these kings had borne toward the Buddha the same thirsting admiration. Hearing that King Udayana's statue was complete, they all rejoiced and came together to King Udayana's place, each bringing quantities of flowers, incense, and music to offer to the Buddha statue, and presenting precious jewels to King Udayana. All the kings said: “Your deed is truly rare — it has drawn out the poisoned arrow of sorrow for us all.”}

{align:justify|Meanwhile, in the Trāyastriṃśa Heaven, the Tathāgata had expounded the sūtras and Dharma for his mother[16], and the gods all gained joyful benefit. What was to be done, the Buddha had done. The Buddha then told the heavenly hosts:}

{align:justify|- O young gods! The Buddhas, the World-Honored Ones, possess an ever-abiding body. If there are beings fit to be liberated, the Buddhas manifest themselves to teach and preach the Dharma to them. When the work is finished and none remain who can receive the teaching, the Tathāgata manifests no more. The unwise suppose the Buddha truly passes into extinction. But the body of the Tathāgatas is the Dharma-body, an eternal body, never truly extinguished.}

{align:justify|- O young gods! Such is the Dharma of all Buddhas: to liberate beings they appear — or appear not.}

{align:justify|Next the Buddha taught on, saying:}

{align:justify|- Know this! Among the gods here, those fit to be liberated I have liberated. Now I am about to descend to Jambudvīpa. You gods who think of me, be diligent and earnest; do not be heedless.}

{align:justify|Why? — For it is the offense of heedlessness that keeps you from the holy path of supreme awakening. Yet because in the distant past you planted wholesome roots, you now dwell here enjoying heavenly wealth and bliss[17], only to grow heedless, intoxicated with pleasure, cultivating no blessed deeds. These delights follow impermanence; once they fall away, you drift forever along the evil paths.}

{align:justify|- Further, O gods! Your afflictions are most heavy[18]: seeing any superior, you give rise to envy, never reflecting that whatever heaven's joys are higher come from creating more blessed karma — if I diligently cultivate, I too shall attain them. Now your forms are bright and lustrous as the sun risen at dawn; yet if you harbor envy and jealousy, you shall turn dark as spent ashes and cinders, and fall into pitch-black hells, unable even to see your own hand — thereafter to become hungry ghosts feeding on filth!}

{align:justify|- Further, O gods! Enjoying the blessed rewards of pure, dignified bodies and mighty, valiant power, through envy you shall take female bodies, utterly losing the virile strength and valor of true men.}

{align:justify|- O young gods! I recall that in ancient times countless kings suffered unjust ruin through your very envy.}

{align:justify|O young gods! Long ago there was an asura king[19] named Ô La, who practiced austerities with pure observance of precepts. You gods dispatched a celestial maiden named O Ba Thi to seduce the king's heart and make him lose his virtue. The king grew stained, his august virtue waned, and he was slain by Nārāyaṇa, while countless asura hosts perished with him. Having slain this king, Nārāyaṇa went on to exterminate the asura hosts and brought the maiden O Ba Thi back to the heavenly palace.}

{align:justify|- There was also a king named Na Ha Thọ, whom you gods beguiled with words into aiding the heavenly hosts against the asuras; once the asuras were broken, you gods turned and destroyed him!}

{align:justify|- Further, O gods! Over the Lady Śacī[20] you conceived jealousy; Śacī egged the king into quarrel, causing the sage A Gia Ba, though innocent, to be suspected and to utter a dreadful curse.}

{align:justify|- Further, O gods! You once deceived King Ế Trà, telling him: “Where those sages dwell there is much pure gold.” The king believed these words and forced the sages to yield it; the sages therefore conceived wrath, and at once fierce fire consumed the sage-king.}

{align:justify|- Long ago there was also a king named Đề Bà, who often held great assemblies to gain offerings; through this blessed karma his power was free and sovereign, and he ascended to this very heaven to enjoy heavenly pleasures. You gods, harboring envy, caused Đề Bà to fall from Trāyastriṃśa down to the human world, all his majesty shattered — like the moon gone dim, like a river run dry.}

{align:justify|- O young gods! In this world many possess august virtue and mastery, or attain supernormal powers; some accomplish the four bases of supernormal power[21]... yet should a single envious thought arise, such merit falls away all at once — as with Devadatta, who in dense delusion conceived envy toward me and at once lost his five supernormal powers.}

{align:justify|Then the Lord of Heaven addressed the Buddha:}

{align:justify|- World-Honored One! I now have a doubt I wish to ask about: what exactly is envy?}

{align:justify|Śakra asked on:}

{align:justify|- World-Honored One! If a being sees another's superiority and thinks: “How can I obtain what they have obtained” — is such a thought envy?}

{align:justify|The Buddha replied:}

{align:justify|- No! That is a covetous mind, not envy[22].}

{align:justify|- Śakra, Lord of Gods! Envy means seeking fame and gain for oneself, begrudging others their possession — hating and resenting what others have. That is envy.}

{align:justify|Then all the heavenly hosts rose from their seats, knelt with faces to the ground, joined their palms toward the Buddha, and addressed him:}

{align:justify|- As the Buddha teaches, we gods shall all obey. O Tathāgata, honored by the world, our Father, our Lord, most venerable, most victorious: toward us you have aroused great compassion, coming hither so that all the gods might benefit. Yet our wish remains unfulfilled; we would once more invite the Buddha in one request:}

{align:justify|- World-Honored One! People of the world below slight us gods. Why? Because all Buddhas are born among humans[23], and in the human realm attain right awakening. Among humans there are arhats who have realized the fruit. Solitary realizers of great august virtue also appear in the midst of humankind.}

{align:justify|If the Tathāgata now leaves this place to descend to Jambudvīpa, worldly people will all suppose that we gods do not know the Tathāgata's great august virtue, worthy of receiving the gods' rightful offerings, and will further suppose: “They do not know how to make offerings to the world's honored Tathāgatas. May the Tathāgata linger a little while to accept our small offerings, that worldly people may know the gods make offerings to the Buddha.”}

{align:justify|Then the Buddha silently accepted.}

{align:justify|The Buddha then told the venerable Mahāmaudgalyāyana:}

{align:justify|- Go swiftly ahead down to Jambudvīpa, inquire after the four assemblies, and speak thus: “All who remember me should gather at the land of Sāṃkāśya; within seven days they shall see me.”}

{align:justify|Maudgalyāyana bowed at the Buddha's feet, and in the blink of an eye arrived in Jambudvīpa[24], delivering the Buddha's message to the four assemblies. King Udayana and all the people, hearing the Buddha's tidings, leapt with joy in body and mind, all sorrow dispelled, all at ease.}

{align:justify|Then the four assemblies — bhikkhus and bhikkhunīs, laymen and laywomen — wishing to journey together to the land of Sāṃkāśya, first gathered in the city of Rājagṛha[25] and deliberated:}

{align:justify|- The Tathāgata, honored by the world, is about to descend to Jambudvīpa. Whoever first reverently venerates the Buddha shall be foremost during the time before the Dharma vanishes.}

{align:justify|The venerable Mahākātyāyana, hearing this, was inwardly displeased, fearing the bhikkhunīs might become foremost. Why? Among the whole order of nuns were Uu Ba Ban Da and Utpalavarṇā — two bhikkhunīs versed in the treasury of all Buddhas' Dharma, accomplished in supernormal powers, whom none but the venerable Mahāmaudgalyāyana could match. Reflecting so, Kātyāyana in every way rebuked the bhikkhunī assembly.}

{align:justify|Then the bhikkhunī Utpalavarṇā told the nuns:}

{align:justify|- We women, amid worldly life, are commonly held in honor: though our lineage be lowly, we are still revered by men and furnished with every provision. Moreover, the nuns in the Buddha's teaching mostly come of royal and ministerial kin. Our nuns are zealous, uphold the precepts, never violate decorum, and are replete with virtuous accomplishments — yet still we must bow to bhikkhus newly ordained. Now the venerable Kātyāyana rebukes us on many counts... For the nuns' sake I shall display skillful means, making the bhikkhunīs excel him. Having spoken so, she journeyed with the four assemblies[26] together to Sāṃkāśya.}

{align:justify|Then King Prasenajit, King Ajātaśatru, and King Nghiêm Xí of Vaiśālī... each king led four divisions of mighty troops[27] as escort, in great power. The elephants and horses they rode were adorned with precious things; banners, canopies, incense, flowers, and music lent them august and stately bearing, their aspect like that of gods — and all journeyed to the city of Sāṃkāśya.}

{align:justify|King Udayana then arrayed his four divisions as attendants. Mounted upon a white elephant caparisoned with jewels and embroidery, the king himself conveyed the Buddha statue he had made. Strewing flowers, bearing banners, with musicians playing in offering-train, he set out from his own land toward the city of Sāṃkāśya.}

{align:justify|Then the god Viśvakarman, together with the heavenly hosts, knowing the Buddha was about to descend to Jambudvīpa, fashioned three jeweled stairways from the city of Sāṃkāśya up to Trāyastriṃśa: the middle stair of beryl, the two side stairs of gold, the footrests laid with white silver, inlaid with the seven heavenly jewels.}

{align:justify|The heavenly lord Śakra sent messengers to the Yāma Heaven, the Tuṣita Heaven, the Nirmāṇarati Heaven, the Paranirmitavaśavartin Heaven, and the Brahmā Heavens[28], announcing: “The Buddha is about to descend to Jambudvīpa; if you wish to make offerings, come down to this place.” He further sent messengers to the Heaven of the Four Kings, the Dragon Kings of the great ocean, the king of Gandharvas, the king of Kiṃnaras, and the Yakṣa hosts, telling them: “Now the World-Honored One is about to descend to Jambudvīpa; bring your offerings hither.”}

{align:justify|Then the gods, dragons, spirits, and the rest, hearing the tidings, all gathered in Trāyastriṃśa.}

{align:justify|Then the World-Honored One, atop Mount Sumeru together with the vast hosts of gods from all heavens[29], was about to descend; all the gods filling the sky before and behind, their august virtue blazing, their radiance shining like the full moon amid space ringed with myriad stars, like the sun newly risen in dazzling brilliance. The Buddha and his assembly in that hour were just so.}

{align:justify|In that very hour, through the Buddha's spiritual power, five rarities appeared in Jambudvīpa:}

{align:justify|First, the gods there perceived none of the unclean things of the human realm.}

{align:justify|Second, women who saw the heavenly youths conceived no thought of lustful attachment.}

{align:justify|Third, men who saw the celestial maidens gave rise to no defiled thought.}

{align:justify|Fourth, earthly people from afar clearly beheld the offerings of the heavenly hosts.}

{align:justify|Fifth, the gods' bodies, bright, pure, and beautiful, are unseen by worldly people — yet through the Buddha's spiritual power, humans could see them with perfect clarity.}

{align:justify|When the Buddha stepped from the heavenly palace onto the jeweled middle stair, the god Brahmā stood at his right holding a white parasol, and Śakra at his left holding a white whisk. The remaining gods, abiding in empty space, followed the Buddha down, all sounding every kind of music. Gods bore gemmed banners and canopies, strewing flowers in offering. Gods of the Śuddhāvāsa heavens crowded the sky; hundreds of thousands of gods and goddesses wearing necklaces of jewels together sang the Buddha's holy virtues. Other gods in mid-sky scattered fragrant incense and sweet blossoms. The dragon spirits rained down light perfumed showers. The sky then was cloudless, and gentle thunder delighted all who heard. Gandharva and Kiṃnara spirits played the unsurpassed, exquisite music of the Deva-Nāgas, praising the Tathāgata's deeds of past lives.}

{align:justify|Then, in Jambudvīpa, kings, ministers, common folk, and the four assemblies filled the whole city of Sāṃkāśya: strewing fragrant flowers, bearing canopies, blowing conches, beating drums, all kinds of music, they offered reverence skyward. Hands raised, palms joined, they gazed up at the Buddha. Famous blossoms of heaven and earth, above and below mingling, fell thick to knee-deep; heterodox followers witnessing these things all gave rise to refuge and reverence[30].}

{align:justify|When the World-Honored One stepped upon the jeweled stair and had descended halfway, the Four Heavenly Kings at their stations arrayed offerings to the Buddha — offerings of supreme, exquisite rarity, unknown among humans since ancient times.}

{align:justify|The World-Honored One accepted the Four Heavenly Kings' offerings, then descended with the great assembly down every step to the lowest one. As the Buddha was about to set foot upon the earth, the bhikkhunī Utpalavarṇā transformed herself into a wheel-turning king[31], leading four divisions of troops and the seven treasures to the fore, swiftly descending from mid-sky to the Buddha's place. The kings of the lands all wondered: “Whence comes this wheel-turning king?”}

{align:justify|Then the venerable Subhūti, dwelling in his room, saw the Buddha descending and at once adjusted his robes to bow in reverence, while the bhikkhunī Utpalavarṇā shed her wheel-king body, resumed her former shape, and hastened to bow at the World-Honored One's feet.}

{align:justify|Then the Buddha roundly rebuked that bhikkhunī, telling her:}

{align:justify|- Do you not know? Subhūti venerated me first! Who told you to transform into a wheel-king? Admitted to the ranks of the renunciate and fully ordained, you have already received more than your share. Your wisdom is petty, yet your deceit is boundless; though you would repay kindness with compassion, it amounts to but a drop of dew. How could you become foremost in my Dharma?}

{align:justify|Hearing the Buddha's admonition, Utpalavarṇā was deeply ashamed, and addressed the Buddha:}

{align:justify|- World-Honored One! I now know my offenses are not few. From this day forth, I dare display supernormal powers no more.}

{align:justify|Then, in Jambudvīpa, the kings of the lands, the ministers, and the four assemblies all presented the offerings they had brought to the Buddha.}

{align:justify|King Udayana, bearing the Buddha statue upon his head[32] along with the finest rare offerings, approached the Tathāgata and presented them. The Buddha's body, replete with fine marks, stately, peerless, and radiant amid the gods, was like the full moon emerging from clouds. The carved Buddha statue, compared with the Buddha, was as an anthill beside Mount Sumeru — no comparison possible; only the curled hair and the gem-like tuft faintly resembled the Buddha, enough for the four assemblies to know it as A BUDDHA STATUE.}

{align:justify|King Udayana addressed the Buddha:}

{align:justify|- I bow to the World-Honored One! The Tathāgata in former times, amid birth and death, seeking awakening, practiced measureless, boundless hardships, difficult to practice, difficult to endure[33], and thereby attained a body most wondrous, beyond compare. The statue I made resembles the Buddha not at all; I venture to consider myself gravely at fault.}

{align:justify|The World-Honored One told King Udayana:}

{align:justify|- You incur no fault! You have now benefited the measureless, and none can compare with you. In my teaching you are now the first exemplar. Through these causes, countless beings of later ages shall give rise to the deepest faith. You have now gained measureless blessing and vast wholesome roots.}

{align:justify|Then the heavenly king Śakra addressed King Udayana:}

{align:justify|- O King, here and now have no fear. When the Tathāgata dwelt before in heaven and in this human world, he praised the merit of your statue-making, and the heavenly hosts greatly rejoiced in sympathy. Believers of later ages shall all, thanks to you, make Buddha statues and enjoy blessings. Now, King, rejoice and celebrate.}

{align:center|(END OF THE UPPER FASCICLE)}

{align:center|**THE MAHĀYĀNA SŪTRA ON THE MERIT OF MAKING BUDDHA IMAGES**}



{align:center|**Under the Great Tang Dynasty: The Land of Khotan**}



{align:center|**Rendered into Vietnamese by Bhikkhu Thích Thiện Thông of Vietnam**}

{align:justify|At that time, the Buddha sat upon the lion throne[35] at the sanctuary in the land of Sāṃkāśya. In the hearts of the four assemblies, each one thought: “We long to hear the Tathāgata expound the merit of making statues. If a being fashions one, what blessings does such a one reap?”}

{align:justify|Then the Great Bodhisattva Maitreya, discerning their thoughts, rose from his seat, bared his right shoulder, knelt upright with palms joined, and addressed the Buddha:}

{align:justify|- World-Honored One! King Udayana made a Buddha image. Whether the Buddha is in the world or after his nirvāṇa, if a believer fashions a Buddha image according to their share, what merit does such a one reap? We humbly beg the World-Honored One to expound these marks at length.}

{align:justify|The Buddha told the Bodhisattva Maitreya:}

{align:justify|- Maitreya, listen and bear it well in mind; I shall speak for you.}

{align:justify|- If there is a good man or good woman of pure faith, wholly intent in mindfulness upon the Buddha's virtues, ever contemplating the Tathāgata's sovereign august virtue, complete with the ten powers, the four fearlessnesses[36], the eighteen exclusive attributes, great kindness, great compassion, all-aspect knowledge, the thirty-two marks of a great man, and the eighty minor characteristics — every pore emitting infinite lights of varied hues, adorned and perfected by hundreds of thousands of millions of surpassing blessings, radiant and unobstructed with measureless wisdom, measureless concentrations, measureless supernormal powers... all such virtues measureless, free of faults, beyond compare.}

{align:justify|Whoever contemplates thus and gives rise to profound faith and delight, and fashions a Buddha statue according to those fine marks — that person's merit is vast and boundless, beyond reckoning.}

{align:justify|- Maitreya! If someone embroiders a Buddha image with silk threads and yarns[37], or casts one in gold, silver, copper, iron, lead, or zinc, or carves one from fragrant sandalwood, or fashions one from pearls, shells, brocade and woven silks, red earth, cement, plaster, clay, or various woods... using such materials according to their means to make a Buddha statue[38], even one as small as a thumb, so that others who see it know it is a venerable statue — that person's blessed reward I shall now describe:}

{align:justify|- Maitreya! Such persons, though still drifting through birth and death, will never be born into desperately poor families, nor into lowly or ignoble lineages, nor lonely and remote on a country's fringes. Nor will they be born into wrong-view families, traders' or merchants' homes, or butchers' households, down even to lowly lines, impure brothels, or the households of heterodox ascetics holding wrong views — unless through the power of compassionate vows; apart from that, they will never be born into such homes.}

{align:justify|Such a person is often born into the clan of a righteous wheel-turning king of great power, or into a Brahmin line of pure conduct, or into a wealthy house free of grave offenses. Wherever born, they always meet Buddhas and reverently serve and make offerings to them; or they become kings who safeguard the true Dharma, teaching it to others without going against the path; or mighty wheel-turning sage-kings, perfected in the seven treasures, endowed with a thousand sons, flying through the sky to teach the four continents, free and blissful all their lives. Or they become Śakra, kings of the Yāma Heaven, kings of the Tuṣita Heaven, kings of the Nirmāṇarati Heaven, kings of the Paranirmitavaśavartin Heaven — heavenly and human delights, none unenjoyed. Such blessed rewards continue unbroken. Wherever born, they are always men, never taking a woman's body, nor the base hermaphrodite or dual-sex body. The body they receive at birth is never ugly: eyes never blind or defective, ears never deaf or misshapen, noses never crooked or broken, mouths never twisted, lips never drooping nor pursed, teeth never gapped, missing, black, or yellow, tongues never short or drawn back, necks never goitered, backs never hunched, skin never blotchy, hands never stubby, feet never lame — never too gaunt, never too stout, neither too tall nor too short... none of the unlovely features at all.}

{align:justify|That person's body is upright and dignified, face full and round. Hair deep blue, soft, bright, and clean. Lips red as lacquer, eyes like blue lotuses, tongue broad and long. Teeth white, even, and close; speech skillful and fine, delighting all who hear. Elbows well-proportioned, palms level, back and thighs full, shoulders and chest broad, hands and feet soft as cotton floss, all marks complete with nothing lacking, sinews most strong — like the god Nārāyaṇa[39] himself.}

{align:justify|- Maitreya! Suppose a man falls into a cesspit, then climbs out, shaves away the filth, washes himself clean with water, anoints his body with perfumes, and dons fresh clean robes... Between such a one and one still stuck in the cesspit, not yet out — how far apart are they in cleanliness and fragrance? World-Honored One, the distance is incalculably great!}

{align:justify|- Maitreya! If someone still amid birth and death gives rise to faith and fashions a Buddha image, the distance between that person's state after fashioning and before is just the same. Know that such a one, in life after life, is cleansed of karmic hindrances; all manner of arts they master without being taught. Though born human, their faculties are as those of the heavens. If born in heaven, they far surpass the many gods. Wherever born, they are free of deformity — never leprous, never cancerous, never possessed by demons and sprites, never seized by madness, consumption, jaundice, malaria, stones, foul sores, deep scars, unremitting dysentery, indigestion, aching joints, or hemiplegia... such four hundred and four maladies, none at all[40].}

{align:justify|Nor are they harmed by poisons, weapons, staves, tigers, wolves, lions, flood, fire, thieves, or robbers — no such sudden calamities can injure them. Ever fearless, they commit no offenses.}

{align:justify|- Maitreya! If beings formerly created evil karma and must undergo all manner of torment — clamped, manacled, cangued, chained, beaten, scolded, burned, branded, flayed, scalped, hung upside down, even disemboweled and burned alive... should they give rise to faith and fashion a Buddha image, none of those painful fruits shall they undergo. They shall never be born where brigands rage, cities collapse, baleful stars work transformations, famine and pestilence strike... to such places they shall never be born; to say they are born there would be false speech.}

{align:justify|Then the Great Bodhisattva Maitreya addressed the Buddha:}

{align:justify|- World-Honored One! The Tathāgata always says: “Good and evil karma are never lost.[41]” If beings create grave karma and must be born into base, lowly lines — poor, deformed, short-lived — then later give rise to faith and fashion Buddha statues, must they still undergo those sinful fruits, or undergo them not?}

{align:justify|The Buddha told the Bodhisattva Maitreya:}

{align:justify|- Now listen carefully; I shall speak for you. If beings who created offenses then resolve to fashion statues, seek repentance, firmly cut off the evil, and vow never to transgress again, then the offenses' creation and blessings alike wipe them away. Now I shall illustrate this matter at length for you.}

{align:justify|- Maitreya! Suppose a man, formerly miserly, through those causes suffers poverty — without wealth, lacking necessities — when suddenly he meets a bhikkhu fresh from deep concentration; the man at once offers food and drink in alms, and having given, leaves poverty behind; whatever he seeks accords with his wish. Maitreya! Does that poor man's former evil karma and due retribution still remain?}

{align:justify|Maitreya replied:}

{align:justify|- World-Honored One! Through offering food, his old evil karma is wholly destroyed; he leaves poverty behind and gains wealth in abundance.}

{align:justify|The Buddha taught:}

{align:justify|- Bodhisattva Maitreya! Just as you say. Know that the other is just the same: through fashioning a statue, old evil karma is ended utterly, nothing remaining; what was due to undergo, they undergo no more.}

{align:justify|- Maitreya! There are three kinds of karma[42]: first, retribution undergone in this very life; second, retribution undergone in the next life; third, retribution whose fruit ripens only after many lives... Of these three kinds, each may be fixed or unfixed. For one who with faith fashions a Buddha statue, only fixed karma is partially undergone in this present life; all other kinds are never undergone.}

{align:justify|Then the Bodhisattva Maitreya addressed him:}

{align:justify|- World-Honored One! The Tathāgata always says there are five deeds of the gravest weight, certain to plunge one into the uninterrupted hell[43]: killing one's father, harming one's mother, killing an arhat, maliciously shedding a Buddha's blood, and breaking the harmony of the sangha. If beings who formerly created such offenses later give rise to faith in the Buddha and fashion statues, do such persons still fall into hell, or fall not?}

{align:justify|The Buddha told the Bodhisattva Maitreya:}

{align:justify|- Now I shall tell you another parable: Suppose a man with a mighty bow amid a forest aims upward and looses at the leaves; the arrow flies straight through, unhindered. If beings who committed those transgressions later fashion Buddha statues and sincerely repent, gaining faith not yet fully firm, and imagine their offenses grown thin and light — then even should they fall into hell, they emerge at once, like the arrow shot straight through without pause; so it is with such persons... Or like a bhikkhu accomplished in supernormal power, passing from this shore across the sea around all four continents with nothing to bar him. So it is with them: through former offenses they briefly fall into hell, yet that former karma cannot obstruct them.}

{align:justify|The Bodhisattva Maitreya questioned further:}

{align:justify|- World-Honored One! The Buddhas, the Tathāgatas, possess a Dharma-nature body, not a body of form and marks. If form and marks were taken as the Buddha's body, then the bhikkhu Nanda and wheel-turning kings would rightly be Buddhas, for they too possess all the fine marks. If a being violates the Buddha's Dharma-body[44] — calling Dharma non-Dharma and non-Dharma Dharma — then later gives rise to faith and fashions a Buddha statue, is that grave offense destroyed together, or destroyed not?}

{align:justify|The Buddha told the Bodhisattva Maitreya:}

{align:justify|- If a being calls Dharma non-Dharma and non-Dharma Dharma, using only the mouth without corrupting right view, then later gives rise to faith and delight and fashions a Buddha statue, the former evil karma meets only light retribution in the present body; they fall not into evil paths, though from birth and death they are not at once liberated.}

{align:justify|The Bodhisattva Maitreya questioned further:}

{align:justify|- World-Honored One! If someone steals property from a Buddha stūpa, steals goods of the permanent sangha, steals goods of the sangha of the four quarters or of the present sangha — consuming them personally, giving them to others to consume, treating them as their own. The World-Honored One always says: “Consuming goods stolen from a Buddha stūpa or from the sangha is a most grave offense.[45]” If beings who created such offenses then deeply reproach themselves, give rise to pure faith, and fashion Buddha statues, are the offenses of such persons destroyed?}

{align:justify|The Buddha told the Bodhisattva Maitreya:}

{align:justify|- If beings who consumed such goods then reflect, embrace shame and remorse, restore the usual amount, and vow never to transgress again, now I shall tell you a parable: As a poor man formerly deep in debt who suddenly finds a buried treasure of countless gold and jewels — his debts repaid in full with great surplus remaining. So it is with such offenders: having restored those goods and further fashioned a Buddha statue, they escape calamitous suffering and ever abide in peace.}

{align:justify|Then the Great Bodhisattva Maitreya again addressed the Buddha:}

{align:justify|- World-Honored One! As the Buddha once taught: “One who commits an expulsion offense within the Buddha's teaching[46] is not called alive.” If someone who committed such an offense then recollects the Buddhas' virtues and fashions a statue, within the Buddha's teaching does such a one “live” again? And in this life, or the second, third, or fourth life, may they realize the Dharma?}

{align:justify|The Buddha told the Bodhisattva Maitreya:}

{align:justify|- It is as with a man bound and fettered, hands, feet, and neck — should he be freed, like a bird escaped from the net flying off at will; so it is with the other. Should they give rise to faith, contemplate the Buddhas' virtues, and fashion a Buddha statue, all karmic hindrances are wiped away; amid birth and death they swiftly emerge, unobstructed. Maitreya, know that there are three vehicle Dharmas[47]: the Śrāvaka vehicle, the Pratyekabuddha vehicle, and the Great Buddha Vehicle. Makers of Buddha statues, according to whichever vehicle they aspire and incline toward, in that very vehicle attain liberation. And if they seek only to accomplish the Buddha path, craving no other reward, then though hindrances be heavy they are swiftly destroyed; though amid birth and death, no calamitous suffering befalls them, until they realize supreme awakening, gain a pure land, possess all fine marks, and enjoy a lifespan long and unending.}

{align:justify|Then in the assembly, those who had not yet aroused the Great Vehicle mind all conceived doubts, thinking: “Did our Tathāgata in past times fashion Buddha statues or not? If he did, why was his lifespan beset with impurities, not pure and clean?”}

{align:justify|King Prasenajit, through the Buddha's spiritual power[48], at once rose from his seat, joined his palms, knelt upright, and addressed the Buddha:}

{align:justify|- World-Honored One! I see the Tathāgata's faculties, fine marks, and lineage are all foremost; in my heart I am resolved, with no room for doubt. Yet the Tathāgata once had a khadira thorn pierce his foot. Once Devadatta rolled a boulder whose shard drew the Buddha's blood. Once before he declared himself ill and summoned Jīvaka to prepare fine medicine. Once he suffered pain in the back and told the venerable Mahākāśyapa to recite the seven factors of awakening, whereupon the pain was dispelled. Once he suffered aching pain and sent Ānanda to a Brahmin house to beg cow's milk. Once before, at the village of Ta La, for the three months of the rains retreat he ate only horse-feed barley. And once he begged for alms and got nothing, returning with an empty bowl.[49]”}

{align:justify|- World-Honored One! As the World-Honored One says: “If anyone fashions a Buddha statue, all karmic hindrances are wiped away, leaving all suffering and sickness behind, with no afflictions.” Did the World-Honored One in former kalpas fashion statues or not? If in the past he fashioned statues, what causes explain those events?}

{align:justify|The Buddha told King Prasenajit:}

{align:justify|- Listen! Listen and bear it well in mind; I shall analyze and explain for the great king. O great king! In former kalpas I, seeking awakening, used precious jewels, fragrant sandalwood, paint, and drawings to fashion Buddha statues — more than all the gods and humans in this present assembly. Through that blessing, though still coursing through birth and death undeluded, I received a body durable as diamond, indestructible.}

{align:justify|Further, O great king! I recall that countless kalpas ago, still amid birth and death, when I fashioned Buddha statues I was still beset with greed, anger, and delusion[50]... measureless afflictions joined with my mind — yet never for a single thought-moment did I, through offenses, suffer the four elements in disarray, or possession by demons and spirits, or the least affliction of sickness, while of necessities not a single thing was ever lacking — how much less now that I have realized the path of awakening? How could there still be matters “not as wished”?}

{align:justify|- Great king! Had I in former times fashioned Buddha statues, and now some residual karma of retribution remained to undergo, how could I utter the fearless proclamation, declaring: “Fashioning statues unfailingly wipes away all evil karma”?}

{align:justify|- Great king! In past times I gave alms of measureless food, drink, wealth, and jewels[51]; how could I in this life beg and get nothing, reduced to eating horse-feed? If such a thing were really so today, how could I in countless sūtras praise the perfection of giving in many ways, saying the blessings of generosity never prove false?}

{align:justify|- Great king! I am one who speaks truth, speaks reality, never speaks to deceive. If I ever spoke falsely, whom could I liberate?}

{align:justify|- Great king! Long ago I ended all evil karma, abandoned what is hard to abandon, did what is hard to do, gave up life and limb hundreds of thousands of millions of times, fashioned measureless images of Buddhas, and repented measureless evil offenses — how could I suffer wounds, sickness, horse-feed fare, and hunger? Had I once gained supreme fruits only to lose them now, how could I urge the cultivation of those wholesome blessings?}

{align:justify|- O great king! The Buddhas, the Tathāgatas, possess an eternal body, a Dharma-nature body[52]; to liberate beings they manifest the above events — they are not real. Pierced feet, aching backs, begging milk, taking medicine... down to entering great nirvāṇa, dividing their relics among many places, building stūpas, and so on... all are the Buddhas' fine skillful means, letting beings behold such marks.}

{align:justify|Great king! That I display such sicknesses in the world aims to let beings know clearly that karmic retribution is never lost, making them fearful so they cut off offenses and cultivate wholesome deeds — and thereafter come to know the eternal body, know the Dharma-nature body, its lifespan boundless, its land pure.}

{align:justify|- Great king! The Buddhas, the Tathāgatas, never speak falsely; all is great compassion and skillful wisdom, and therefore they can in every way manifest thus.}

{align:justify|Hearing these words, King Prasenajit leapt with joy, and together with countless hundreds of thousands of beings, all aroused the mind of the supreme path[53].}

{align:justify|Then the Great Bodhisattva Maitreya continued questioning the Buddha:}

{align:justify|- World-Honored One! There are women of narrow will, prone to jealousy, anger, frivolity, flattery, and deceit, nursing grudges unrelinquished, ungrateful for kindness received. Though they seek awakening, few keep a lasting resolve; ever wishing to delude everyone, they are in turn deluded by others[54].}

{align:justify|- World-Honored One! If such a woman fashions a Buddha statue, are those deeds wiped away? Can she become one who knows and repays kindness? Can she be perfected in wisdom, great kindness, and great compassion? Toward birth and death, can she grow weary of it? Apart from vow-power, shall she ever take a woman's body again? Can she become like Gautamī and the Buddha's mother, Lady Māyā?}

{align:justify|The Buddha answered the Bodhisattva Maitreya:}

{align:justify|- If a woman fashions a Buddha statue, she shall surely never take a female body again. Should she take one, it shall be as the noblest, foremost of women. Yet women possess five virtues, and women who fashion statues gain them beyond all womankind. What are the five virtues?}

{align:justify|First: bearing sons and daughters.}

{align:justify|Second: noble lineage.}

{align:justify|Third: a chaste and virtuous nature.}

{align:justify|Fourth: beauty of both substance and features.}

{align:justify|Fifth: a graceful and perfect figure.}

{align:justify|- Maitreya! All women, through eight causes, ever take female bodies for many lives. What are the eight?}

{align:justify|First: cherishing the female body.}

{align:justify|Second: craving the sensual pleasures of womanhood.}

{align:justify|Third: praising with the mouth the fair form of women.}

{align:justify|Fourth: a crooked heart, hiding one's deeds.}

{align:justify|Fifth: despising one's husband.}

{align:justify|Sixth: a heavy heart, longing for another.}

{align:justify|Seventh: knowing kindness received yet turning against it.}

{align:justify|Eighth: adorning oneself with guile, longing to entrance men.}

{align:justify|Should they utterly cut off these eight and fashion Buddha statues, until becoming Buddhas they shall ever be men; taking a woman's body again is out of the question.}

{align:justify|- Maitreya! There are four causes by which men take women's bodies. What are the four?[55]}

{align:justify|First: mocking Buddhas, Bodhisattvas, and holy ones in a woman's voice.}

{align:justify|Second: slandering precept-keepers, calling them precept-breakers.}

{align:justify|Third: delighting in flattery to deceive others.}

{align:justify|Fourth: envying whoever surpasses them.}

{align:justify|Should any man do these four, after death he shall surely take a woman's body, besides enduring measureless suffering in the evil destinies. Should he give rise to deep faith, repent former deeds, and fashion a Buddha statue, such offenses are all destroyed, and never again shall he take a woman's body.}

{align:justify|- Maitreya! There are four causes by which men take hermaphrodite bodies[56]. What are the four?}

{align:justify|First: maiming the forms of humans and beasts.}

{align:justify|Second: raging in anger, mocking and slandering śramaṇas who keep pure precepts.}

{align:justify|Third: abounding in lust, deliberately breaking precepts.}

{align:justify|Fourth: keeping company with precept-breakers and urging others to break them.}

{align:justify|Should any man formerly do these, then give rise to faith and fashion a Buddha statue, until becoming a Buddha he shall never undergo such retribution, ever a man with faculties complete.}

{align:justify|Maitreya! There are four deeds by which men take dual-sex bodies[57], lowest among all people. What are the four?}

{align:justify|First: lewd defilement in venerable precincts.}

{align:justify|Second: though in a man's body, stained indulgence with men.}

{align:justify|Third: performing sexual acts upon oneself.}

{align:justify|Fourth: trading in female beauty for others.}

{align:justify|Should any being do these things, deeply reproach themselves, repent former transgressions, give rise to pure faith, and fashion a Buddha statue, until becoming a Buddha they shall never take such a body.}

{align:justify|Maitreya! There are further four conditions by which men ever conceive a woman's lust in their hearts[58], delighting when others treat them as men treat women. What are the four?}

{align:justify|First: doubting or jesting to slander others.}

{align:justify|Second: delighting in women's dress and adornment.}

{align:justify|Third: lewd indulgence with kinswomen.}

{align:justify|Fourth: truly without virtue, yet accepting others' homage.}

{align:justify|Through these causes men give rise to such aberrant afflictions. Should they repent offenses committed, commit no new ones, and give rise to faith and delight to fashion a Buddha statue, those offenses are destroyed and those minds likewise ended.}

{align:justify|Maitreya! There are five kinds of stinginess[59] that ruin beings. What are the five?}

{align:justify|First: begrudging one's village and neighborhood haunts — hence born in desolate, lonely wilds.}

{align:justify|Second: begrudging shelter under one's eaves — hence becoming a worm of the privy, ever dwelling amid foul excrement.}

{align:justify|Third: begrudging one's fair beauty — hence a form unsightly, against one's wish.}

{align:justify|Fourth: begrudging one's goods and wealth — hence poor and needy, lacking clothes and food.}

{align:justify|Fifth: begrudging the Dharma one knows — hence the fruits of a dull, brutish birth.}

{align:justify|Should they repent their former deeds and fashion a Buddha statue, they shall leave that former stingy mind behind and never undergo such fruits as described!}

{align:justify|Maitreya! There are further five conditions by which beings are born among barbarians, in border marches[60], or in ages without the Buddhadharma. What are the five?}

{align:justify|First: giving rise to no pure faith toward the blessed field of the Three Jewels.}

{align:justify|Second: against truth and awry of right principle, yet presuming to instruct.}

{align:justify|Third: teaching and transmitting to others what accords not with truth.}

{align:justify|Fourth: breaking the sangha's harmony, splitting it in two.}

{align:justify|Fifth: down to the least — setting two bhikkhus at odds, breaking their harmony.}

{align:justify|Cutting off the above deeds and fashioning Buddha statues, they shall ever meet Buddhas and ever hear the essential Dharma.}

{align:justify|Maitreya! Beings have further five causes for being ever loathed and driven off by the many, until even kin dislike meeting them[61]. What are the five?}

{align:justify|First: double-tongued speech.}

{align:justify|Second: harsh and cruel speech.}

{align:justify|Third: much quarreling.}

{align:justify|Fourth: much resentment.}

{align:justify|Fifth: skill in innuendo aimed at disparaging others.}

{align:justify|Should they later resolve to fashion Buddha statues, repent old deeds, and vow to create them no more, that person's offenses are all wiped away, and all people cherish and draw near them. Why? Because the Buddhas possess measureless, boundless surpassing blessings, measureless, boundless vast wisdom, measureless, boundless concentrations and liberations[62] — replete with rare Dharma-nature virtues.}

{align:justify|- O good men! Suppose one ground a trichiliocosm of worlds to fine dust, then ground that dust again, dividing every mote into as many motes as the dust of one great chiliocosm — how many multiplied motes of those world-grounds could there be! Suppose one took a ground mote and, by supernormal power, traveled eastward, in one instant crossing as many worlds as the ground dust of one great chiliocosm (a billionth part); in the second instant, the third instant, just the same, journeying on through as many kalpas as that ground dust; and in every instant of every kalpa, crossing as many worlds as the ground dust of a whole great chiliocosm; having crossed those worlds, dropping one mote. Then returning for another mote, journeying eastward past even more worlds to drop it once more; with the third mote surpassing the last, and so on in turn, doubling each time, until all the ground dust is exhausted.}

{align:justify|As with the east, so with the south, west, and north. The lands of the four quarters that traveler crossed — all those worlds ground wholly to dust. All beings together reckoning these dust-motes might still know their number; yet the virtues of each single hair of the Tathāgata's body can never be known. Why?}

{align:justify|Because the virtues the Buddhas possess are all measureless and inconceivable.}

{align:justify|O good men! Suppose as many people as those dust-motes just described, each endowed with Śāriputra's wisdom — all their wisdom together would not equal one thought-moment of the Tathāgata's wisdom. Why?}

{align:justify|Because in every thought-moment the Tathāgata ever manifests right concentrations, liberations, retentive dhāraṇīs, and the like — more numerous than the dust of the parable — each replete with measureless surpassing virtues.}

{align:justify|The surpassing virtues of the Buddhas — all Śrāvakas and all Pratyekabuddhas cannot even comprehend their names to call them! Therefore whoever with pure faith fashions a Buddha statue sees every karmic hindrance destroyed, gains measureless, boundless virtues, until realizing supreme awakening and wholly uprooting suffering for all beings.}

{align:justify|Having spoken this sūtra, the Buddha was met with joy: the Bodhisattva Maitreya and Śakra, Lord of Gods, King Udayana, and the whole world of gods, humans, asuras, and Gandharva spirits, hearing the Buddha's teaching, all rejoiced, believed, accepted, and practiced accordingly.}

{align:center|END OF THE LOWER FASCICLE}
